---
// src/pages/what-is-yini-and-why.astro
import CONFIG from '../config/conf'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

/*
    NOTE: This page is intended for very first time users:
    - That has Zero context of what YINI is.
    - Even more focused than the front page (where users probably has been forwarden from somewhere else).

    1. What YINI is
    2. Why it exists
    3. Why they should care
    4. How to try it in under 60 seconds
 */
---

<BaseLayout
    headTitle="What is YINI and Why?"
    headDescription="YINI is a human-friendly, text-based configuration format — less verbose than JSON, simpler than YAML, and more expressive than INI."
>
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-2 text-center">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
            What is YINI and Why?
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base md:text-lg">
            YINI is a human-friendly, text-based configuration format — less
            verbose than JSON, simpler than YAML, and more expressive than INI.
        </p>
        <p>
            A calm, predictable way to write configuration and structured data
            for real-world projects.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
            <a href="/get-started" class="btn btn-primary"> Get Started </a>

            <!-- !!Temp disabled
            <a href="/playground" class="btn btn-outline"> Open Playground </a>
            -->

            <a href="/yini-code-examples" class="inline-block underline">
                View Examples
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="why-yini pb-2">
        <h2>❓ What is YINI?</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>YINI</strong> is an INI-inspired, text-based data format designed
            for humans first.
        </p>
        It combines:
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                the simplicity of <strong>INI</strong>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                the structure of <strong>JSON</strong>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                the flexibility people <i>want</i> from <strong>YAML</strong>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-2 pb-6">
        <!-- <h2>📝 Example</h2> -->
        <h3>Example of YINI code:</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="/samples/basic.yini.png"
                ><img
                    class="mt-0 mb-1 rounded-lg border-2 border-black/50"
                    src="/samples/basic.yini.png"
                    alt="File basic.yini"
                /></a
            >
            Source file: <a
                href="/samples/basic.yini"
                target="_blank"
                onclick="track('basic.yiniClicked')">basic.yini</a
            >
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-2 pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">💡 What can I do with YINI?</h2>
        Build, convert, and consume structured configuration and data with simple
        tools.
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">🛠 Convert YINI → JSON via CLI.</li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                📦 Load YINI into your app as native objects.
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                🧩 Use YINI for configuration, data files, pipelines, and
                tooling.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-2 pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">🧭 Why should I care?</h2>
        <p>
            Because configuration lives for years, not minutes. YINI is designed
            to stay readable, predictable, and safe as projects grow.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-2 pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">🧯 Why does YINI exist?</h2>
        Most config formats fail as projects grow.
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>INI</strong> becomes flat and unstructured
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>JSON</strong> becomes noisy and hard to edit
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>YAML</strong> becomes fragile and unpredictable
            </li>
        </ul>
        YINI was created to fix that.
    </section>

    <section class="mt-2 pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">✨ What makes YINI different?</h2>
        <ul>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Clear structure without indentation rules</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">No fragile whitespace semantics.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Readable nesting that scales</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
                    Files remain easy to scan even when large.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Predictable parsing</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">Defined by a formal grammar and spec.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Human-first design</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">Comments, consistency, minimal noise.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Balanced design</strong>
                <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
                    More structure than INI, less noise than JSON, fewer edge
                    cases than YAML.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-4">
        <h2 class="mt-5">⚖️ Quick comparison</h2>

        <h3>JSON (harder to read & maintain at scale) vs YINI</h3>
        <h3>JSON:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "service": {
    "id": "aurora-gateway",
    "environment": "prod",
    "telemetry": {
      "samplingRate": 0.15,
      "exporter": "otlp",
      "endpoint": "http://otel-collector:4317"
    },
    "http": {
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": 9000,
      "trustedProxies": ["10.0.0.0/8", "192.168.0.0/16"]
    },
    "limits": {
      "requestsPerMinute": 1200,
      "burst": 60,
      "maxBodyKb": 512
    },
    "webhooks": {
      "enabled": true,
      "targets": [
        {
          "name": "alerts",
          "url": "https://hooks.example.com/alerts",
          "secret": "****"
        },
        {
          "name": "billing",
          "url": "https://hooks.example.com/billing",
          "secret": "****"
        }
      ]
    },
    "_comment": "JSON can't do comments; people add fields like this (or separate docs)."
  }
}`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong>YINI keeps the config readable and self-documented</strong>
            (real comments), with clear structure <strong
                >without the JSON noise</strong
            >. And large JSON can be annoying to maintain.
        </p>

        <h3>YINI (same config, cleaner + real comments):</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yini">{`/* Aurora Gateway service configuration (human-edited) */

^ Service
id          = "aurora-gateway"
environment = "prod"

^^ Telemetry
samplingRate = 0.15          // 0.0 - 1.0
exporter     = "otlp"
endpoint     = "http://otel-collector:4317"

^^ Http
listen         = "0.0.0.0"
port           = 9000
trustedProxies = ["10.0.0.0/8", "192.168.0.0/16"]

^^ Limits
requestsPerMinute = 1200
burst             = 60
maxBodyKb         = 512

^^ Webhooks
enabled = true

targets = [
  { name: "alerts",  url: "https://hooks.example.com/alerts",  secret: "****" },
  { name: "billing", url: "https://hooks.example.com/billing", secret: "****" }
]`}</code></pre>

        <h3>"YAML foot-gun" vs YINI predictability (tabs/indentation)</h3>
        <h3>YAML:</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yaml">{`# One stray indent changes the structure
app:
  name: My App
  server:
    host: 127.0.0.1
    port: 8080
   allowedOrigins:   # <- off by one indent (hard to spot)
    - https://myapp.com
    - "http://localhost:3000"`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong
                >YAML uses indentation to define structure; YINI uses explicit
                section markers.</strong
            >
            This makes large configs easier to scan — and much harder to break accidentally.
        </p>

        <h3>YINI (same config):</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-yini">{`# Structure is defined by markers; indentation is optional
^ App
name = "My App"

^^ Server
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 8080
allowedOrigins = ["https://myapp.com", "http://localhost:3000"]
`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            <strong>Point:</strong> In YINI the hierarchy is signaled by markers,
            not whitespace.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-5">Quick comparison table</h3>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>YINI</th>
                        <th>INI</th>
                        <th>JSON</th>
                        <th>YAML</th>
                        <th>TOML</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>

                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Human-friendly by default</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Readability at scale</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Predictable parsing</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Formal grammar / spec</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Nested structure</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Comments</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Minimal syntax noise</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>

                    <!-- Added rows (useful for ad/landing-page visitors) -->
                    <tr>
                        <td>Designed for configuration</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Clear error diagnostics</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Safe for large configs</td>
                        <td>✅</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                        <td>❌</td>
                        <td>➖</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quick Start -->
    <div class="try-under-a-minute full-bleed pr-8">
        <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl pt-1 pb-6">
            <h2>⚡ Try YINI in under a minute</h2>
            <blockquote>No build step. No installation. No setup.</blockquote>
            <ul class="space-y-6">
                <li>
                    Save the two lines below as <code>config.yini</code>: <i
                    ></i><br />
                    <pre><code class="language-yini">{`^ App
name = "Hello"`}</code></pre>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>From your terminal, run this:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">npx yini-cli parse --pretty config.yini</code></pre>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>Results in JSON:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
    "App": {
        "name": "Hello"
    }
}`}</code></pre>
                </li>
            </ul>
            That's it.
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
                You just parsed your first YINI file.
            </p>
        </section>
    </div>

    <section class="pb-0">
        <h2>🧪 Real example</h2>
        <h3>YINI code:</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="/samples/settings.yini.png"
                ><img
                    class="mt-0 mb-1 rounded-lg border-2 border-black/90"
                    src="/samples/settings.yini.png"
                    alt="File settings.yini"
                /></a
            >
            Source file: <a
                href="/samples/settings.yini"
                target="_blank"
                onclick="track('settings.yiniClicked')">settings.yini</a
            >
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
            This example uses features from the official YINI specification.
        </p>

        <h3>Parsed output (JSON):</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
    "Settings": {
        "serviceId": "NebulaService",
        "release": "3.2.1",
        "debugMode": false,
        "tagline": "\"Nebula\" is a cloud of gas and dust in outer space.",
        "Network": {
            "bindAddress": "127.0.0.1",
            "bindPort": 8080,
            "allowedOrigins": [
                "https://myapp.com",
                "http://localhost:3000"
            ]
        },
        "Capabilities": {
            "enableSearch": true,
            "experimental": [
                "new-ui",
                "streaming-api"
            ],
            "pools": {
                "min_units": 2,
                "max_units": 5,
                "size_mb": 128,
                "timeout_sec": 90
            }
        },
        "DB Config": {
            "host": "db.internal",
            "ssl": true,
            "Security": {
                "username": "service_user",
                "password": "****"
            }
        }
    }
}`}</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
            New to YINI? Read a short <a
                href="/intro-yini-config-format"
                class="underline">intro to the YINI format</a
            >.
        </p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <section>
        <h2>🔭 Next steps</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Get Started
                    PURPOSE: Immediate action
                    EFFECT:  Momentum
                    */
                }
                <a href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.getStarted.url}>Get Started</a
                ><br />
                <p class="mt-2">
                    Learn how to install and parse your first config in minutes.
                </p>
            </li>
            <!--li>
            <a href="/yini-playground">Try it Online</a><br />
            <p class="mt-2">Try it in the Playground.</p>
        </li-->
            <li>
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Code Examples
                    PURPOSE: Visual reinforcement
                    EFFECT:  Trust
                    */
                }
                <a href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.examples.url}>Code Examples</a
                ><br />
                <p class="mt-2">
                    Real-world YINI examples showing common configuration
                    patterns.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                {
                    /*
                    STEP:    Specification
                    PURPOSE: Technical validation
                    EFFECT:  Commitment
                    */
                }
                <a href={CONFIG.siteLinks.hero.spec.url}>Specification</a><br />
                <p class="mt-2">
                    Official YINI format rules with full technical details of
                    the YINI format.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a
                    href={CONFIG.urls.external.gitHub.yiniOrg}
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-slate-300 no-underline hover:text-sky-300 hover:underline"
                    >YINI on GitHub ↗</a
                >
                <p class="mt-2">Official GitHub page.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4">
            <strong>
                <!-- Note: Wait a bit with below line-->
                <!--YINI is open-source, actively maintained, and already used in real
            projects.-->
                YINI is open-source and actively maintained.</strong
            >
        </p>
    </section>
</BaseLayout>
